			<div class="content" id="post-content-2394634">
									

<p>Indeed! Except the poor, poor spiny anteater.</p>
<p>I actually agree with Breezeblock, the bolded/capitalized/first-in-each-line letters trick is pretty meh. The action was nice and ambiguous up to that point, but the note at least partially solidified what was until that point a delicate dance.</p>
<p>I love the scale of the anomaly and I think it's fantastically written, but the ending might just be enough to move me from a strong upvote to a no-vote.</p>
<p>Edit: For more specificity:<br />
What I liked in the article was the implication that a collection of ravens, through their natural curiosity (or perhaps grief, which they definitely <a href="https://kcts9.org/programs/in-close/science/do-crows-mourn">also display in bizarre ways</a>), had simply decided that it would be interesting to be a human for a while. This, balanced against the back-and-forth of dreaming and the life of the original Jessica, made for a nice push-and-pull.</p>
<p>Having them spell out their collective will, in plain English (even if it is &quot;hidden&quot;, bolded text isn't subliminal) really nails them down to an at least human-like mind, and sucks a lot of the alien-ness inherent to the animal mind out of it.</p>
<p>I know the article is incredibly successful and that others enjoyed the gambit, but it just sapped it for me.</p>

							</div>

								
